.TH GIT-PARALLEL 1 "November 2026" "git-parallel 2.0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
git-parallel \- execute Git or other commands across multiple repositories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git parallel
.RI [ options ] " -- " git-command " [ arguments ...]"
.br
.B git parallel
.RI [ options ] " -- ::: " command " [ arguments ...]"
.br
.B git parallel
.RI [ options ] " --list"
.br
.B git parallel
.RI [ options ] " --freeze " repository
.br
.B git parallel
.BR --help | --version
.PP
The standalone executable
.B git-parallel
accepts the same arguments as
.BR "git parallel" .
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git-parallel
locates Git working trees below a starting directory and runs a command
in each selected repository.  It can run multiple commands concurrently,
collect their output separately, and print the results in repository order.
.PP
By default, the command following
.B --
is interpreted as arguments to
.BR git (1).
Prefix the command with
.B :::
to run an executable directly instead.  Each command runs with its
working directory set to the corresponding repository.
.PP
The default concurrency is two jobs.  Execution proceeds in batches of
up to the configured job count.  All jobs in a batch are waited for before
their captured output is printed and the next batch begins.
.PP
Unless
.B --strict
is specified, failures of commands in individual repositories do not
make the overall invocation fail.  See
.BR "EXIT STATUS" .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -h , " --help"
Print usage information and exit.  The current implementation returns
status 1 for this operation.
.TP
.BR -V , " --version"
Print the program version and exit.  The current implementation returns
status 1 for this operation.
.TP
.BI -j " count"
.TQ
.BI --jobs " count"
Run up to
.I count
repository commands concurrently.  The default is 2.  The value must
be a positive decimal integer; zero and nonnumeric values are rejected.
.TP
.BI -m " depth"
.TQ
.BI --maxdepth " depth"
Limit the depth of repository discovery using the
.B -maxdepth
predicate of
.BR find (1).
The depth is measured from the starting directory to the discovered
.B .git
entry, not to the repository root.  By default, no depth limit is applied.
.TP
.BI -d " directory"
.TQ
.BI --targets " directory"
Search for repositories under
.I directory
instead of the current working directory.  The long form
.B --target
is also accepted.  Paths are passed to
.BR find (1)
as supplied.
.TP
.BI -e " name"
.TQ
.BI --exclude " name"
Exclude repositories whose directory basename is exactly
.IR name .
This option can be repeated.  Matching is by basename, not by full path
or glob pattern.
.TP
.BR -l , " --list"
List discovered repository paths, one per line, and exit without running
a command.  The current implementation returns status 1 after listing.
.TP
.BI -F " repository"
.TQ
.BI --freeze " repository"
Create or update the file
.I .git-parallel.freeze
in the specified repository, then exit.  The repository must contain a
.B .git
file or directory.  The current implementation returns status 1 after
this operation, including when the path is not a Git repository.
.TP
.BI -c " mode"
.TQ
.BI --color " mode"
Control colored output.  Accepted values are
.BR auto ,
.BR always ,
and
.BR never .
The default is
.BR auto ,
which enables color when standard output is a terminal.  For Git
commands, the selected mode also controls the Git
.B color.ui
setting.  It does not impose a color setting on arbitrary executables.
.TP
.BR -q , " --silent"
Suppress normal execution output, including the initial banner and
captured per-repository output.  Error summaries from
.B --strict
are still written to standard error.
.TP
.BR -v , " --verbose"
Enable normal execution output.  This is the default.  If both
.B --silent
and
.B --verbose
are supplied, the last one processed takes effect.
.TP
.B --strict
Return failure status if any repository command fails.  All selected
repositories are still processed; a failure does not stop the remaining
jobs.  At completion, failed repositories and their individual exit
statuses are reported to standard error.
.SH COMMAND EXECUTION
.SS Git commands
Without the
.B ::: 
prefix, the command is executed through
.BR git (1).
For example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
git parallel -- status --short
git parallel -- fetch --prune
.fi
.RE
.PP
An explicit leading
.B git
in the command is recognized and removed, so
.B "-- git status"
and
.B "-- status"
select the same Git operation.
.SS Other executables
Prefix the command with
.B :::
to invoke a non-Git executable in each repository:
.PP
.RS
.nf
git parallel -- ::: ls -la
git parallel -- ::: make test
.fi
.RE
.PP
Commands are assembled into a single string and subsequently subject
to shell word splitting and pathname expansion.  Arguments are not
preserved as a distinct argument vector, and the command is not evaluated
as a complete shell script.  In particular, shell pipelines, redirections,
and compound expressions should not be assumed to work as they would
with
.BR "sh -c" .
Use an explicit script or shell invocation when shell syntax is needed,
and test quoting carefully, especially for arguments containing spaces.
.SH REPOSITORY DISCOVERY
The starting directory defaults to the current working directory unless
.B --targets
or the corresponding environment variable overrides it.
.B git-parallel
uses
.B "find -L"
to follow symbolic links while searching for entries named
.B .git
that are either directories or regular files.  The parent directory of
each matching entry is treated as a repository.
.PP
A
.B .git
file is supported, including the form used by Git linked worktrees.
Discovery is not restricted to immediate child directories.  Without
.BR --maxdepth ,
all reachable directory levels are searched.
.PP
Discovered repositories are processed in the order returned by
.BR find (1);
no alphabetical sort is performed.  Repositories with the same basename
cannot be distinguished by
.BR --exclude .
.PP
.B --list
shows the discovery result before command execution and does not check
whether a repository is frozen.
.SH PARALLEL EXECUTION AND OUTPUT
For each repository, the program changes to its working directory and
runs the requested command.  Standard error from the command is merged
into its standard output.  Each job writes to a separate temporary file.
.PP
At most the configured number of jobs run concurrently.  The program
waits for every job in the current batch and then prints the captured
files in discovery order.  Output from separate repositories is therefore
not interleaved during normal printing, although the commands execute
concurrently.  A slow job delays printing of the entire batch.
.PP
In verbose mode, output includes an initial invocation banner and a
header identifying the command and repository, followed by command output.
In silent mode, this normal output is suppressed.  With
.BR --strict ,
a final failure report is still emitted on standard error.
.PP
Temporary output files are created below
.B TMPDIR
if set, or
.I /tmp
otherwise, and removed during normal cleanup.
.SH FREEZE FILES
If a repository contains a regular file named
.IR .git-parallel.freeze ,
.B git-parallel
skips command execution for that repository.  It prints a skip notice
in normal output and treats the repository as successful when calculating
strict-mode status.
.PP
Create a freeze marker with:
.PP
.RS
.nf
git parallel --freeze /path/to/repository
.fi
.RE
.PP
To resume execution, remove the marker manually:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rm /path/to/repository/.git-parallel.freeze
.fi
.RE
.PP
The marker is local to the repository working tree.  It is not a Git
configuration setting.  The
.B --list
operation still includes frozen repositories.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal execution completed.  Without
.BR --strict ,
this status is returned even if commands fail in some or all repositories.
With
.BR --strict ,
all executed repository commands succeeded.  Frozen repositories and an
empty discovery result count as success.
.TP
.B 1
A usage or setup error occurred, or at least one repository command
failed under
.BR --strict .
The help, version, list, and freeze operations also currently exit with
status 1; do not interpret that status as evidence that those operations
failed.
.TP
.B 129, 130, 131, 143
With
.BR --strict ,
execution was interrupted by SIGHUP, SIGINT, SIGQUIT, or SIGTERM,
respectively.  Running child processes are terminated and an
interruption message is written to standard error.
.PP
Without
.BR --strict ,
signal handling uses the historical forced-termination behavior rather
than the strict-mode exit-status convention.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The following variable names intentionally retain the spelling
.B DEAULT
used by the implementation.
.TP
.B GIT_PARALLEL_DEAULT_JOBS
Default job count; 2 if unset or empty.
.TP
.B GIT_PARALLEL_DEAULT_MAXDEPTH
Default discovery depth; unlimited if unset or empty.
.TP
.B GIT_PARALLEL_DEAULT_START_DIR
Default starting directory; current working directory if unset or empty.
.TP
.B GIT_PARALLEL_DEAULT_COLOR_MODE
Default color mode; auto if unset or empty.
.TP
.B GIT_PARALLEL_DEAULT_SILENT_MODE
Default output mode; FALSE if unset or empty.  The implementation
suppresses normal output when the value is exactly TRUE.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Parent directory for temporary output files; /tmp if unset or empty.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I repository/.git
Git directory or Git worktree metadata file used to discover repositories.
.TP
.I repository/.git-parallel.freeze
Marker file that prevents command execution in a selected repository.
.TP
.I ${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/git-parallel.XXXXXX
Temporary directory used to capture per-repository output.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Inspect multiple repositories
.PP
.RS
.nf
git parallel -- status --short
.fi
.RE
.SS Fetch updates using four concurrent jobs
.PP
.RS
.nf
git parallel --jobs 4 -- fetch --prune
.fi
.RE
.SS Run tests with strict failure reporting
.PP
.RS
.nf
git parallel --strict -- ::: make test
.fi
.RE
.SS Restrict discovery and exclude a repository
.PP
.RS
.nf
git parallel --targets ./projects --maxdepth 3 \\
    --exclude archived --list
.fi
.RE
.SS Run a Git command quietly and detect failures
.PP
.RS
.nf
git parallel --jobs 2 --silent --strict -- pull --rebase
.fi
.RE
.SS Freeze one repository
.PP
.RS
.nf
git parallel --freeze ./projects/legacy
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
The program operates on multiple repositories and can invoke commands
that modify their working trees or remote state.  Review the discovered
repositories with
.B --list
before running destructive operations.
.PP
The
.B --strict
option changes failure reporting, not scheduling: every selected
repository is still attempted even when an earlier job fails.
